 ## Unreleased
 
+### The engine declares `notation/read` and `notation/analyze`, and a dialog crate supplies their code
+
+[#TBD](https://github.com/dialog-db/dialog-db/pulls), in place of
+[#894](https://github.com/dialog-db/dialog-db/pull/894). The engine now
+declares two more by name and by their cells: a formula `notation/read`
+(a text in, a row a form out) and a resolver `notation/analyze` (a form
+in, a row a thing said of it out). Their implementation is not the
+engine's. The notation supplies it to the environment a query runs in,
+through `Holds`, and a premise asking one finds it there. Both are asked
+in a query of premises that no rule holds.
+
+What this changes for you:
+
+- New: `dialog_query::supplied`, with the traits `NotationRead` and
+  `NotationAnalyze`, `supply_read` and `supply_analyze`, the premises
+  `NotationReadQuery` (`FormulaQuery::NotationRead`) and
+  `NotationAnalyzeQuery` (`ResolverQuery::NotationAnalyze`), and `Facts`,
+  the reads an analyzer makes through the query's environment.
+- New: `dialog_query::query::{PremiseQuery, PremiseRow}`, a query of
+  premises planned as a rule's body is and asked with no rule.
+  `TypeError::UnselectedVariable` refuses one that selects what no
+  premise binds.
+- A premise asking either where the environment holds no implementation
+  fails the query with `EvaluationError::Unsupplied`, naming it, before
+  a row is read. It never answers nothing.
+- A row an implementation gives is held to the cells declared here.
+  `EvaluationError::UndeclaredOutput`, `MissingOutput` and
+  `MistypedOutput` fail the query, naming the formula and the cell.
+- A deductive or inductive rule holding either is refused when it is
+  compiled, built directly or read from data, with
+  `TypeError::SuppliedInRule` (from `AnalysisError::SuppliedInRule`).
+- What an analyzer reads through `Facts` is the query's demand, as a
+  scan's is: a subscription over it is evaluated again when a fact it
+  read changes, and left alone otherwise.
+- `FormulaQuery`, `ResolverQuery`, `TypeError`, `AnalysisError` and
+  `EvaluationError` have new variants. None of them is
+  `non_exhaustive`, so an exhaustive `match` outside this crate needs
+  the arms.
+
+Why: the maintainer, 2026-11-26, wants a query to read a source and
+analyze the form it gives, with neither step storing anything, and the
+point other code reaches the engine through locked down: "define formulas
+in the one crate and then implement them in the other through traits".
+The names and the cells are the engine's, so no crate adds a formula;
+`Holds` is open, so any code holding the environment can supply an
+implementation. A built-in formula is on every replica. One whose
+implementation comes from another crate is only where that crate is
+linked, and what it does would become part of what a stored rule means,
+so no rule holds either yet. Opening rules to them is that refusal
+removed, once that behaviour is versioned in what a replica stores and a
+replica lacking an implementation judges such a rule at rule assembly.
+
+#### Compatibility and migration
+
+- Nothing a replica stores or syncs changes. A rule written as data can
+  name `notation/read` or `notation/analyze`, which now read back, but
+  every rule holding one is refused when it is compiled, so none is
+  stored. An older release does not know either name.
+
